# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: cador-2-multidocument-runtime — N documents, isolated engines, shared blackboard, cooperative coroutine scheduler
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: feature
#+DEPENDS: cador-1-split-nihil-cador
#+BRANCH: cador-multidocument

* Context

Sub-task 2 of the =cador-multidocument-host= epic (§5, §6 C1–C8, and the
analysis document's §4.2 invariant "one runner, N parked contexts").
Developed on its OWN branch (=cador-multidocument=) in parallel with
sub-task 1, per the owner.

* Problem

cador must present a multi-document world to AutoLISP, matching AutoCAD /
BricsCAD MDI semantics, on a single-runner cooperative model — without a
global Lisp environment (C1) and without concurrent per-document
execution (C2).

* Proposal (owner's design, to be specified then implemented)

- **N document images.** Each open drawing is a document with its OWN
  AutoLISP namespace: separate value/function cells, a separate AutoLISP
  engine (evaluation context), and a distinct loaded Lisp program set.
  Switching document switches the active namespace + the active drawing.
- **Shared blackboard, one heap.** A single AutoLISP heap is kept for the
  whole session; the blackboard (=vl-bb-ref= / =vl-bb-set=) is shared
  across documents WITHOUT copying values between per-document heaps —
  there is one heap, so blackboard values are shared by identity, not
  duplicated (values only, never functions — C3).
- **Per-document drawing.** Each namespace owns its drawing (the cador
  entity DB); entity handles / selection sets / ldata are
  document-tagged, cross-document dereference signals (C5).
- **Scheduler = cooperative coroutines.** One thread per document, but run
  as a coroutine (the aldo signal/park handoff model): at most one
  evaluation runs at any instant, any number parked (C2, C8 — no worker
  thread is *required*, but the per-document thread is the coroutine
  carrier). One aldo (debugger) instance per document ⇒ ~2 threads per
  document total (the document runner + its aldo), used cooperatively.
- **Park/resume points.** Reuse the debugger's poll-point / signal-park
  machinery; specify where a document parks and what resuming restores;
  a host call outstanding at a park is the hazard to handle (epic §1.2b).

* Non-negotiable (from the epic §6)

C1 no global env; C2 no concurrent per-document execution; C3 blackboard
values-only; C5 document-tagged handles; C8 single-runner, no mandatory
worker thread.

* Acceptance criteria

- [ ] N documents open concurrently, each with an isolated namespace +
      engine + loaded program set + drawing.
- [ ] Blackboard values shared across documents via the one shared heap,
      no copying; functions rejected.
- [ ] Cooperative scheduler: exactly one running context, others parked;
      document switch parks the current and resumes the target.
- [ ] One aldo instance per document; the coroutine handoff mirrors the
      same-image two-thread debugger design.
- [ ] Cross-document handle dereference signals; per-document drawing
      isolation holds.
- [ ] Conformance probes (E1 symbol identity, E2 blackboard sharing) run
      against cador.

* Notes

Depends on cador-1 (the host must be named cador and own the entity DB
first) and on the D1/D2 specification (cador-3) for the formal
autolisp↔host protocol the scheduler and namespace model realise.

* Implementation plan (proposed 2026-09-13, pending cador-3 sign-off)

Grounded in a full read of the current tree. *The namespace/blackboard half
is already ~80% built in the runtime; the new work is host-side per-document
isolation and the cooperative scheduler.* What exists today:

- =runtime-session= already owns =document-namespaces= (name→namespace), one
  =blackboard-namespace=, =propagated-symbols=, =current-document=
  (=autolisp-runtime/source/model.lisp:193=); =document-namespace= has
  per-document =bindings=/=reactors= and a lifecycle =state=
  (=:loading/:loaded/:active/:inactive/:closing/:closed=, =model.lisp:115=).
- Document switching exists: =register-runtime-session-document=,
  =set-runtime-session-current-document= (=autolisp-runtime/source/api.lisp:632,665=).
- Blackboard shares by identity and rejects functions (C3):
  =blackboard-ref/set= (=api.lisp:1012/1019=), =VL-BB-REF/VL-BB-SET=
  (=autolisp-builtins-core/source/api.lisp:288-304=).
- The =drawing= class is a self-contained, multi-instance-ready DB with
  =COPY-DRAWING= (=clautolisp/drawing/source/model.lisp:71=).
- The park/handoff primitive exists as the debugger's two-thread design
  (=autolisp-debug/source/session.lisp=, =breakpoints.lisp:88-114=:
  =run-debugged-thread=, =queue-hit-handler=, inbound/outbound blocking
  queues, =thread-debug-info=).

The gaps: the =cador= host holds ONE =active-drawing= plus session-global
state (=model.lisp:64=; the code comments already call the next step
"Phase 17f"); there is no cooperative coroutine scheduler outside the
debugger; handles are not document-tagged; E1/E2 probes do not exist; and the
normative =clautolisp-multidocument-analysis.org= (R1–R21/E1–E10) was not
found in this worktree.

** Slice 1 — host holds a set of drawings (the "Phase 17f" the code names)
No scheduler; single-document behaviour stays byte-identical (one entry in the
map). Pure implementation, not spec-gated.
- Replace =cador='s =active-drawing= slot with a =documents= map (keyed by the
  runtime =document-namespace=) + an =active-document= pointer
  (=clautolisp/cador/source/model.lisp:63-69=). The delegation shims
  =cador-entities/-tables/-sysvars/…= (=model.lisp:210-241=) are the single
  seam to re-point at "the active document's drawing".
- Move per-drawing session state (=ename-cache= — already tracks its drawing
  at =:189=; =ldata-store=, =picksets=, =pickfirst=, =open-complex/block=
  handles, iterators) into a per-document record.
- Tag enames/picksets with their owning document; cross-document dereference
  signals (C5).
- Add minimal document-management host generics
  (=host-open-document/host-activate-document/host-document-list=,
  =autolisp-host/source/protocol.lisp=) and have
  =set-runtime-session-current-document= drive =host-activate-document= in
  lock-step (the runtime↔host document link).
- Assert *E1* (symbol identity across documents — already testable via the
  namespace model) and *E2* (blackboard sharing — already true by identity).
- Lands acceptance bullets: N isolated documents, shared blackboard no-copy,
  per-document drawing isolation, C1/C3/C5. Defers C2/C8.

** Slice 2 — cooperative coroutine scheduler (spec-gated on cador-3 §13/D2 I.2)
- Generalise the debugger's inbound/outbound blocking-queue handoff into a
  single-runner scheduler: N parked contexts, at most one running (C2/C8),
  park/resume on document switch.
- Specify and honour the park-with-an-outstanding-host-call hazard (epic
  §1.2b): what a resume restores, and that a host call in flight at a park is
  either forbidden or completed-then-parked.
- One aldo instance per document; the coroutine carrier reuses the two-thread
  design (~2 threads/document, cooperatively).
- Do this AFTER cador-3 pins the formal protocol (this slice realises it).

** Slice 3 — conformance probes + CI
- Land E1/E2 as runnable probes against cador (locate or recreate
  =clautolisp-multidocument-analysis.org='s E-numbering first); wire into the
  test aggregate. Verify with =make -C clautolisp test-sbcl= (full image, per
  the "test the shipped artefact" rule).

** Sequencing / dependencies
Slice 1 can start now (no spec blocker). Slice 2 waits on cador-3 (!192,
under review). Each slice is its own MR with its issue-flow close note; the
epic-level acceptance is met only after Slice 3. This plan is provisional
until cador-3's D2 §I.2 scheduler decision is signed off.

** Slice 1a DONE (2026-09-13) — the host document registry + Group-1 generics

The first landable increment of Slice 1 is in: cador now holds a SET of open
documents with an active pointer, and the D1 Group-1 document-management
generics exist.

- =autolisp-host/source/protocol.lisp=: new generics =host-open-document=,
  =host-close-document=, =host-activate-document=, =host-current-document=,
  =host-document-list=, each with the =:host-not-supported= base fallback
  (so =nihil= correctly refuses them); exported from the package.
- =cador/source/model.lisp=: added the =documents= slot (ordered
  (KEY . DRAWING) alist) and =active-document-key=. =active-drawing= stays the
  current pointer every delegation shim reads, so single-document behaviour is
  unchanged.
- =cador/source/document-api.lisp= (new): =initialize-instance :after= seeds
  the registry with the initial drawing as the first current document; the five
  generics manage it. Uniquifies colliding names; refuses to close the last
  document; activating a closed current document reactivates a remaining one.
- Tests: =cador/tests/document-tests.lisp= (8 tests) — one-current-at-start,
  open-without-switching, name uniquification, activate switches the active
  drawing, activate-unknown signals, *entity-database isolation between
  documents* (E1/C5 core), close-and-reactivate, close-last-refused.
- Verified with the full image: =make -C clautolisp test-sbcl= exit 0.

Deliberately deferred to the next increment(s): migrating per-document
*session* state (picksets, ldata-store, iterators) off the host into the
per-document record; making cross-document handle dereference *signal* (C5's
strong form — today it fails safe: an unknown handle in the current drawing
returns nil, and =ename-cache-drawing= already prevents cross-drawing aliasing);
the runtime =document-namespace= <-> host-document link; and the cooperative
scheduler (Slice 2, spec-gated). cador-2 stays OPEN.

** Slice 2a DONE (2026-09-13) — single-runner scheduler skeleton + host lock-step seam

The first increment of Slice 2 (the cooperative scheduler): the STATE MACHINE
and the runtime->host current-document seam, modeled *without* threads or real
parking. Legitimate because a batch cador run parks nowhere (D2 §I.2), so
continuation capture and the park-with-outstanding-host-call hazard are
deferred with the slots that will carry them.

- =autolisp-runtime/source/scheduler.lisp= (new): =document-scheduler= (ordered
  contexts + a RUNNING pointer) and =scheduled-context= (context, document-key,
  status :runnable/:running/:parked, plus reserved CONTINUATION / PENDING-BREAK
  / SAVED-ENV slots for later slices). =scheduler-register-context=,
  =scheduler-context-list=, =scheduler-current-context=, and =scheduler-activate=
  — the core transition that demotes the previous runner, promotes the target,
  installs its evaluation context, and switches the runtime current document in
  lock-step. =%assert-single-runner= is the executable form of C2 (signals
  :scheduler-invariant if two contexts are ever :running). No thread is created
  — the debugger's two-thread rendezvous is the hosted case; cador's model is
  continuation capture (R21).
- =autolisp-runtime/source/model.lisp=: added =scheduler= slot to
  =runtime-session= and =host-document-key= to =document-namespace=, both nil by
  default (single-document sessions unchanged).
- =autolisp-runtime/source/api.lisp=: =*document-activation-hook*= (nil by
  default) + a guarded call in =set-runtime-session-current-document=, the
  dependency-inversion seam a host/driver layer installs to activate the
  matching host document — the runtime never names cador. Returns the document.
- Tests: =autolisp-runtime/tests/scheduler-tests.lisp= (5) — register/list,
  single-runner enforcement across activations, activation follows the current
  document, the activation hook fires in lock-step (hook restored via
  unwind-protect), and the no-regression guard (a fresh session has no
  scheduler and the hook is nil).
- Verified: =make -C clautolisp test-sbcl= exit 0.

Deferred to Slice 2b/next: INSTALLING the hook in the cador/driver layer (so a
real runtime document switch drives =host-activate-document=) and its
cross-system integration test; then real continuation-capture parking, the
per-context dynamic-env save/restore, and the deferred-break discipline for the
outstanding-host-call hazard; then Slice 3 (E1/E2 probes, wire host).

** Slice 2b DONE (2026-09-13) — the runtime->host lock-step is now wired

The =*document-activation-hook*= seam from 2a is now INSTALLED, so a runtime
document switch actually drives the host.

- =autolisp-host/source/nihil.lisp=: installs, at load (beside the existing
  =*default-runtime-host*= / =*host-command-function*= installs), a
  host-agnostic =*document-activation-hook*= —
  =activate-host-document-for-namespace=: when the switched-to document carries
  a =host-document-key= and the session carries a host, it calls the generic
  =host-activate-document=. A no-op for single-document / host-less sessions
  (key nil), so installing it globally is safe; it uses only the generic, so it
  works for any host that implements activation (cador today).
- Test (=cador/tests/document-tests.lisp=):
  =runtime-current-document-switch-drives-cador-active-drawing= — two runtime
  namespaces linked to two cador documents, in a session whose host is that
  cador; switching the runtime current document flips
  =cador-active-drawing= to the matching document, both ways. Proves the seam
  end-to-end.
- Fixed a cross-suite hazard 2b exposed: the 2a scheduler hook-test had
  =setf=-then-restore-to-nil on =*document-activation-hook*=, which — now that
  the host layer installs a real hook — would clobber it for every later suite.
  =%with-fresh-active-context= now REBINDS the hook (and the active context), so
  the installed hook survives; the =single-document-session-has-no-scheduler=
  guard no longer asserts the hook is nil.
- Verified: =make -C clautolisp test-sbcl= exit 0.

Remaining for the scheduler proper: real continuation-capture parking, the
per-context dynamic-env save/restore, and the deferred-break discipline; then
Slice 3.

** Slice 2c DONE (2026-09-13) — per-context saved-env round-trip + deferred-break

The two self-contained, synchronously-testable halves of the park/resume
contract, without real continuation capture (a batch cador run parks nowhere,
D2 §I.2).

- =autolisp-runtime/source/scheduler.lisp=: =%scheduler-save-env= /
  =%scheduler-restore-env= wired into =scheduler-activate= — the demoted runner
  saves its per-context runtime globals into =saved-env=, the promoted context
  restores them. Slice 2c carries exactly one such global, =*current-form*= (a
  per-evaluation runtime global that lives OUTSIDE the evaluation-context);
  everything already inside the context / namespace (current doc/ns, =*error*=,
  dynamic-frame) keeps riding the existing =*active-evaluation-context*= swap. A
  nil =saved-env= (a never-run context) restores nothing, so first activation
  and the single-document path are untouched.
- Deferred-break discipline (=pending-break=): =scheduler-request-break= sets
  the flag on a target (or the running context); =scheduler-observe-break= is
  the poll point — it clears and reports the running context's break once, and
  a break on a non-running context is NOT observed until that context is
  activated (the deferral). No async interrupt: this is the cooperative half of
  the outstanding-host-call hazard (D1 §13.5).
- Tests (=autolisp-runtime/tests/scheduler-tests.lisp=, +4): save/restore
  round-trip of =*current-form*= across switches; restore is a no-op for a
  never-run context; request-break on target and on the running context;
  observe clears-once and defers a non-running context's break. The test macro
  now also rebinds =*current-form*= so nothing leaks.
- Verified: =make -C clautolisp test-sbcl= exit 0.

Explicitly deferred to Slice 3: real continuation capture (the =continuation=
slot); per-document CMDECHO (host/session-scoped today — belongs behind
=host-activate-document=, not the runtime scheduler); vl-enable-user-cancel
(no state exists to save until the builtin stores a flag); and the per-document
correlation stack (§13.4 — not built; lands with the §13 park/resume protocol).

** Slice 3a DONE (2026-09-13) — E1/E2 conformance probes against cador

The remaining cador-2 acceptance criterion (conformance probes E1 symbol
identity, E2 blackboard sharing) is met as deterministic FiveAM tests. E1/E2
