- Pure runtime properties (per-document namespaces + the one per-session
  blackboard), so they live in the runtime suite; cador holds drawings, not
  lisp namespaces. Hermetic — most use explicit contexts / namespaces, no
  global state; the one switch test uses the existing rebind macro.
- Verified: =make -C clautolisp test-sbcl= exit 0.

Found while writing E1/E2 (flagged, deferred): *C3 is not enforced* —
=blackboard-set= / =builtin-vl-bb-set= do not reject a function value, though
D2 §I.5 decides they must. A =callable-value-p= guard in those two is a small
separate code change (a "Slice 3b" candidate), NOT added here so E1/E2 stay
pure-observation.

** Slice 3b DONE (2026-09-13) — C3 blackboard function-rejection enforced

- =autolisp-builtins-core/source/api.lisp= =builtin-vl-bb-set=: after the
  symbol-arg check, reject a callable value (=callable-value-p= — subr, user
  function, or lambda-form) with :blackboard-function-value. Enforces C3 / D2
  §I.5 "the blackboard is values-only; storing a function signals."
- =callable-value-p= exported from autolisp-runtime + imported into
  builtins-core.
- Test =vl-bb-set-rejects-a-function-value-c3= (builtin-tests.lisp): a plain
  value stores; a lambda-form value signals :blackboard-function-value.
- Verified: =make -C clautolisp test-sbcl= exit 0.

** Slice 3 boundary — what remains needs cador-4's interactive/wire host

Batch-completable Slice 3 work done: E1/E2 (3a), C3 (3b). Still headless-doable
and candidates for follow-up increments: per-document CMDECHO (host-side, behind
=host-activate-document=) and =vl-enable-user-cancel= per-context state (the
builtin is a no-op today). But two Slice-3 items are STRUCTURALLY gated on an
interactive/out-of-process host (cador-4), not deferrable-by-choice:
- *Real continuation-capture parking.* The spec models park = continuation
  capture (D2 §I.2). Common Lisp has no portable first-class continuations, and
  C8/R21 forbid cador from REQUIRING a worker thread (the debugger's two-thread
  handoff is the hosted case only). A batch cador run has no park points at all
  (D2 §I.2). So real parking has no in-image realization; it arrives with the
  wire host, where suspension is the round-trip itself.
- *The §13.4 correlation stack* rides in with that same §13 park/resume wire
  protocol.
These belong to cador-4 (the epic's wire-host sub-task), not cador-2's batch
scheduler. cador-2 stays OPEN pending that decision.

** Slice 3c DONE (2026-09-13) — per-document sysvars confirmed

Per-document CMDECHO turned out to need no code: =cador-sysvar= reads
=(cador-sysvars host)= = the ACTIVE drawing's header variables
(=cador/source/sysvars.lisp:126=), so since slice 1a made each document its own
drawing, every sysvar (CMDECHO included) is already per-document. Added
=cador-sysvars-are-per-document= (=cador/tests/document-tests.lisp=): CMDECHO=0
set in document A does not leak to B and survives a round-trip through B.
=make -C clautolisp test-sbcl= exit 0. =vl-enable-user-cancel= is a deliberate
no-op (SIGINT-based, holds no state by design), so there is nothing to make
per-document there.

** CORRECTION (pjb, 2026-09-13) — real parking is NOT wire-host-gated

The "Slice 3 boundary" note above is superseded. pjb: *our continuations are
our threads, parked on a condition variable* — a parked context is a thread
blocked on its condition; to resume it, notify that condition and have the
current thread wait on its own. That is the debugger's blocking-queue
rendezvous (=autolisp-debug .../session.lisp=, =breakpoints.lisp= bq-push /
bq-pop), and the condvar handoff itself enforces at-most-one-running (C2/C8 —
the per-document thread is the cooperative coroutine carrier, not a concurrent
worker). So real continuation-capture parking IS implementable in-image and is
back IN SCOPE for cador-2 (thread + condition-variable handoff), being designed
now. The correlation stack still follows the §13 park/resume protocol. cador-2
is NOT being closed with parking deferred.
